          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            %em chaotic cosmic inflation
            (
            %a{:href => "#Linde83"}> Linde 83
            ) or
            %em
              chaotic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eternal+inflation"} eternal inflation
            is one variant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} physics
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
          %p
            The name comes about since the model naturally predicts an ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            in which local regions stochastically undergo
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiverse"} multiverse
            (but see the caveats there).
          %p
            While the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            in general is in excellent agreement with astrophysical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            is disfavored by recent measurements which show low upper bounds on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            (
            %a{:href => "#PlanckCollaboration13"}> PlanckCollaboration 13
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BICEPKeckPlanck15"} BICEP2-Keck-Planck 15
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            instead prefer the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Starobinsky+model+of+cosmic+inflation"} Starobinsky model of cosmic inflation
          %h2#description Description
          %p
            Despite its name and its consequences, chaotic inflation is arguably the most uncontrived model of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Linder02"} Linde 02, section II
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/potential"} potential
            term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of the hypothetical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inflaton+field"} inflaton field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            simply to be parabolic
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi m
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 2
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V(\phi) \coloneqq \frac{m_\phi^2}{2} \phi^2
          %p
            hence to be the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            term of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E2+interaction"}> phi^2 interaction
            ).
          %p
            Whereas other inflationary models assume a more monotonely decreasing potential with and assume in an
            %em ad hoc
            way that the inflaton field is to have a large amplitude “at the big bang” to then slowly “roll down” (at each point in spacetime, being a field!) its potential, the idea of (
            %a{:href => "#Linde83"}> Linde 83
            ) is that instead the inflaton by and large sits in its potential minimum in equilibrium, but that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+fluctuations"} quantum fluctuations
            stochastically (“chaotically”) drive it out of its minimum here and there. Wherever this happens
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            sets in and blows up the region of the ambient spacetime in which the inflaton happened to have fluctuated out of its equilibrium.
          %p
            This idea of “chaotic” inflation driven by vacuum fluctuations of the inflator is in fact not tied to the simple square potential as above, and various variants have been considered, such as a
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"} Higgs field
          %h2#implications Implications
          %p
            The idea of eternal cosmic inflation has been argued to provide a possible way to conceptualize the measured values of dimensionless “physical constants”, such as the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fine+structure+constant"} fine structure constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yukawa+couplings"} Yukawa couplings
            and notably the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            these parameters are not really constants but are dynamical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            that just happen to have constant value (“moduli fields”) over large scales, then they might, so the argument, still vary from one “inflationary bubble” to the next. Thereby the idea of eternal inflation combined with that of a fundamental theory that has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+fields"} moduli fields
            (such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            with its
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiverse"} multiverse
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article is
          %ul
            %li#Linde83
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrei+Linde"}> Andrei Linde
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Chaotic Inflation,
          %p Reviews include
          %ul
            %li#Linder02
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrei+Linde"}> Andrei Linde
                ,
                %em Inflationary Theory versus Ekpyrotic/Cyclic Scenario
                (
